 BOM part is what was intended. A 24k value would give a bleed path 1000 times weaker than the part that was sourced.\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(comp (ref \\\"R36\\\") (value \\\"24k\\\") ... (description \\\" 125mW Thick Film Resistors 150V \u00b1100ppm/\u2103 \u00b15% 24\u03a9 0805 Chip Resistor - Surface Mount ROHS\\\")\"},\n        {\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(field (name \\\"MPN\\\") \\\"FRC0805J240TSD\\\")\"},\n        {\"source\": \"spice\", \"quote\": \"R36 VOUTN VOUTP 24k\"}\n      ],\n      \"impact\": \"Depending on which value is correct, the output across VOUTP/VOUTN either has a much heavier load than intended (24 ohm) or an unintended, much weaker bleed (24k). This affects the output's discharge and measurement behaviour.\",\n      \"confidence\": 0.4\n    }\n  ],\n  \"summary\": \"R62 is a 10 ohm pull-up on the e-stop buffer output, while its own MPN and LCSC fields and the neighbouring pull-ups indicate 10k, which is a clear value error. R36 also has a value (24k) that conflicts with its 24 ohm MPN and description, which is a lower-confidence second finding.\"\n}",
